---
title: BoosterTutor, After the Email
blurb: 17Lands asked me to stop fetching drafts, so the input I built everything on is gone. Here's what replaced it, plus practice drafts, a second set, and why the Tomes stay free.
date: 2026-10-22
tags: [web]
---

Phase 6 wrapped up yesterday, so accounts are real now. You can sign in with email or
Google, save a draft as a Study, and find it later in My Studies. That also means
Gnarl finally comes out from behind the admin key: anyone with an account gets two
Tomes a day to unlock his notes. I was planning to write about that, but then an email
showed up and changed the shape of the whole project, so this post is mostly about
that instead.

## 17Lands said no

Two posts ago I explained how BoosterTutor would fetch one user's draft from 17Lands
when they pasted the link. I wrote it down as an accepted risk, with a note that the whole
thing would be withdrawn if 17Lands objected. I'd emailed them to ask, and on October 21 they
answered. The public datasets are fine to use with attribution, but any automated
access to the site is not, and they named draft replays specifically. They're dealing
with a lot of unauthorized traffic, and it's holding back how often they can publish
the public data that tools like mine depend on.

That's a pretty clear answer, and honestly a fair one. My first instinct was to keep
the code around behind a switch in case they ever offered an official export. Then I
realized there were no users yet, so a switch would just be dead code pretending to be
a plan. So the link parser, the endpoint, the rate limits and every line of copy about
pasting a link are all deleted, and the cached drafts were erased along with them. The
17Lands credit for the statistics stays, since those still come from their public
data. The lesson I'm taking away is to ask before building on someone else's site, not
after.

## Practice drafts

Losing the link left Draft Review with no way in, which made Phase 7 a lot more
urgent. Practice Draft has been running in my DraftDouble prototype for weeks, and now
it lives in BoosterTutor. You sit down at a table with seven bots, open real packs
pulled from 17Lands' public draft data, and draft all 42 picks. Once you're done, you
get the same review as before, verdicts and all, and you can unlock Gnarl's Notes with
a Tome.

The bots are deliberately imperfect. Across about 1.5 million human picks where a
`questionable` card was available, people took it 18% of the time, so the bots do the
same. I also ran realism checks comparing bot tables to human ones, and the bots fail
all three. Humans crowd into five color pairs, while the bots spread evenly across all
ten. I'm keeping them anyway. They're there to pass you believable packs, not to
imitate a Platinum player's taste in archetypes, and now the numbers that show how far
off they are are written down instead of guessed.

Every practice draft is also pinned to a Stats Snapshot, a frozen copy of the format's
numbers at the moment you started. If the stats update tomorrow, your draft's verdicts
don't shift under you. That's what makes Redrafts work: you can replay any practice
draft from pick one with the same packs, and the bots repeat their original picks until
your choices change what they see. Redrafts branch into a little tree under the
original in My Studies, so you can try the line you didn't take and see what happens.

Before turning it on, I load-tested it against the live server. Grading a pick takes
about 25 milliseconds of CPU, and a live pick takes about half a second, nearly all of
it network and database round trips. Eight scripted drafts running at once barely
moved the CPU, so the server is in no danger of getting overwhelmed by people drafting
at human speed.

## A second set, and a proper tagger

Then came another surprise: the Hobbit set can't be drafted on Arena anymore. The real
logs I had on my own machine were a Reality Fracture draft and a couple of others, so a
v1 that only reviewed HOB would have nothing to review. Reality Fracture (FRA) now joins
v1, for both Draft Review and Practice Draft.

The rule was that FRA's numbers get measured, not borrowed from HOB. Its land targets,
Deck Ratings and bot behavior all come from its own game data. That paid off right
away. FRA players take a questionable card 21% of the time, not 18%, so the bot rate is
now stored per format. FRA's bots also land much closer to how humans spread across
archetypes, since FRA's players are more evenly split between color pairs to begin
with.

FRA also needed a full card taxonomy, all the tags that feed the synergy term. HOB's
was written in a separate tagger app inside DraftDouble, so I ported it into
BoosterTutor as an admin-only page. It also gained a panel for mana sources, which the
old one never had. Edits stay in the page until I either push the whole set to live in
one transaction, or download the JSON. If anything is off, even one misspelled card
name, nothing gets saved.

## Arena logs are next

The real replacement for the 17Lands link is Arena's own `Player.log`, the file the
game writes on your computer when detailed logging is on. It records every pack you
see and every card you take. It also holds your screen name, account ID, an auth token
and your opponents' names, so I'm being careful with it. The plan is for the browser to
read the file locally and copy out only a short list of fields, the card IDs and pick
numbers. It'll then show you exactly what will be sent, and send only that, while
Python does all the actual parsing. The parser and a set of scrubbed test logs are
already built, and the browser side is what I'm working on next.

## The Tomes stay free

Last thing. I'd planned to have BoosterTutor pay for itself, with a few free Tomes and
then small packs for sale. Then I actually read Wizards of the Coast's Fan Content
Policy, which says fan content can't be put behind a payment. Gnarl's Notes are
commentary on Magic cards, so selling the Tomes that unlock them is selling access.
It does allow donations, which is how tools like 17Lands keep the lights on.

So the Tomes are never for sale. Everyone gets two a day, and BoosterTutor will take
donations through Ko-fi, with no perks for donors. A Tome costs a few cents in Claude
calls, so I think it'll work out fine. I just need to get through the Arena import
first.
